IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ssc-core`

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (United Kingdom), 1992 c. 4.
-- LAYER 1, part ssc6: Part 12ZA, Statutory Paternity Pay (ss 171ZA-171ZK).
-- Lines 3385-3558 of ukpga-1992-4.txt, a plain-text rendering made by a converter script.
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here is "verified against the official text".
--
-- How to read a result (the rule of 2026-10-29, assumed and not ruled by a lawyer):
--   JUST FALSE answers "the text says no in its own words" and carries a comment
--       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "<the quoted words>"
--   NOTHING answers "not covered" and carries a comment  -- NOTHING-kind (<why>)
--   A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- Money is a NUMBER in pounds sterling; a figure is weekly unless its name says otherwise.
-- Numbers that regulations supply ("prescribed") are inputs. Numbers the Act states in its own words
-- (26 weeks, 56 days, 24 weeks of pregnancy) are constants, so that a regulation or order that
-- substituted another number would change the answers.
-- The converter shows "..." inside many sentences of this Part (for example src:3397, 3427, 3445):
-- words repealed from a live provision. The surviving words are encoded.

§ `Part 12ZA -- Statutory Paternity Pay`

-- Helper (it implements no provision): the three-valued AND of a list of answers.
-- JUST FALSE if any answer is JUST FALSE; JUST TRUE if every answer is JUST TRUE; NOTHING otherwise.
-- The JUST FALSE it returns is the "no" of the calling rule, whose own comment quotes the text.
GIVEN answers IS A LIST OF MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZA(1) all of these answers hold` MEANS
    CONSIDER answers
    WHEN EMPTY THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN first FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        CONSIDER first
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN `s 171ZA(1) all of these answers hold` rest
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            CONSIDER `s 171ZA(1) all of these answers hold` rest
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- The Act's own number of weeks of continuous employment, in ss 171ZA(2)(b) and 171ZB(2)(b).
-- src:3393 | (b) that he has been in employed earner’s employment with an employer for a continuous period of at least 26 weeks ending with the relevant week;
@ref s 171ZA(2)(b) (src:3393)
`s 171ZA(2)(b) the number of weeks of continuous employment required` MEANS 26

-- Plain gloss: the Sunday that starts the week a given day falls in (Part 12ZA: a week begins on
-- Sunday unless regulations prescribe another period, s 171ZJ(5)).
-- src:3539 | (5) In this Part, except section 171ZE, “week” means a period of 7 days beginning with Sunday or such other period as may be prescribed in relation to any particular case or class of cases.
@ref s 171ZJ(5) (src:3539)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 171ZJ(5) the first day of the week containing the day` MEANS
    IF `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case`
    THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "or such other period as may be prescribed"
    ELSE JUST (day MINUS ((Day day) MODULO 7))

-- src:3539 | (5) In this Part, except section 171ZE, “week” means a period of 7 days beginning with Sunday or such other period as may be prescribed in relation to any particular case or class of cases.
@ref s 171ZJ(5) (src:3539)
`s 171ZJ(5) the week convention of Part 12ZA` MEANS `a period of 7 days beginning with Sunday or another prescribed period`

-- src:3473 | “week” means any period of seven days.
@ref s 171ZE(11) "week" (src:3473)
`s 171ZE(11) the week convention of section 171ZE` MEANS `any period of 7 days`

§§ `s 171ZA Entitlement: birth`

-- Plain gloss: he satisfies the conditions the regulations prescribe about his relationship with
-- the newborn child and with the child's mother. Those conditions are outside the sources, so
-- whether he satisfies each is a fact the caller states.
-- src:3390 | (a) that he satisfies prescribed conditions—
-- src:3391 | (i) as to relationship with a newborn child, and
-- src:3392 | (ii) as to relationship with the child’s mother;
@ref s 171ZA(2)(a) (src:3390-3392)
GIVEN `he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with a newborn child` IS A BOOLEAN
      `he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with the child's mother` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZA(2)(a) he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with a newborn child and with the child's mother` MEANS
    IF     `he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with a newborn child`
       AND `he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with the child's mother`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are— (a) that he satisfies prescribed conditions"

-- Plain gloss: he has worked for an employer for at least 26 weeks running up to the "relevant
-- week" (the 15th week before the week the baby is due).
-- src:3393 | (b) that he has been in employed earner’s employment with an employer for a continuous period of at least 26 weeks ending with the relevant week;
@ref s 171ZA(2)(b) (src:3393)
GIVEN service IS A `Service for statutory pay`
      `his employment with the employer is employed earner's employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZA(2)(b) he has been in employed earner's employment with an employer for a continuous period of at least 26 weeks ending with the relevant week` MEANS
    IF     `his employment with the employer is employed earner's employment`
       AND service's `weeks of continuous employment with the employer ending with the relevant week`
               AT LEAST `s 171ZA(2)(b) the number of weeks of continuous employment required`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are—"

-- Plain gloss: over the 8 weeks up to the relevant week he earned, on average, at least the lower
-- earnings limit in force at the end of the relevant week (a figure regulations set, so the caller supplies it).
-- src:3394 | (c) that his normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks ending with the relevant week are not less than the lower earnings limit in force under section 5(1)(a) above at the end of the relevant week; and
@ref s 171ZA(2)(c) (src:3394)
GIVEN service IS A `Service for statutory pay`
      `s 5(1)(a) the lower earnings limit in force at the time in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZA(2)(c) his normal weekly earnings are not less than the lower earnings limit at the end of the relevant week` MEANS
    IF service's `normal weekly earnings in pounds over the period the section names`
           AT LEAST `s 5(1)(a) the lower earnings limit in force at the time in pounds`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are—"

-- Plain gloss: he stayed in employment with that employer from the end of the relevant week to
-- the day the child is born.
-- src:3395 | (d) that he has been in employed earner’s employment with the employer by reference to whom the condition in paragraph (b) above is satisfied for a continuous period beginning with the end of the relevant week and ending with the day on which the child is born.
@ref s 171ZA(2)(d) (src:3395)
GIVEN `he has been in employed earner's employment with that employer for a continuous period beginning with the end of the relevant week and ending with the day on which the child is born` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZA(2)(d) he has been in employed earner's employment with that employer from the end of the relevant week to the day the child is born` MEANS
    IF `he has been in employed earner's employment with that employer for a continuous period beginning with the end of the relevant week and ending with the day on which the child is born`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are—"

-- src:3389 | (2) The conditions are—
@ref s 171ZA(2) (src:3389)
GIVEN `condition (a) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `condition (b) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `condition (c) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `condition (d) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZA(2) the conditions are satisfied` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are—"
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): a limb that depends on a figure or fact regulations supply is NOTHING when the caller does not supply it
    `s 171ZA(1) all of these answers hold` (LIST `condition (a) is satisfied`, `condition (b) is satisfied`, `condition (c) is satisfied`, `condition (d) is satisfied`)

-- Plain gloss: where he satisfies the conditions in (2) he is entitled to statutory paternity pay
-- "in accordance with the following provisions of this Part": notice (s 171ZC), the weeks (s 171ZE)
-- and the other bars still apply.
-- src:3388 | (1) Where a person satisfies the conditions in subsection (2) below, he shall be entitled in accordance with the following provisions of this Part to payments to be known as “statutory paternity pay”.
@ref s 171ZA(1) (src:3388)
GIVEN `s 171ZA(2) the conditions are satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZA(1) he is entitled to statutory paternity pay on a birth` MEANS
    `s 171ZA(2) the conditions are satisfied`        -- JUST FALSE here is the "no" of subsection (1): "Where a person satisfies the conditions in subsection (2) below, he shall be entitled" (marginal reading (a): a failed "where" counts as the text's no); the comments of the four conditions quote the text

-- Plain gloss: the "relevant week" is the week immediately before the 14th week before the week
-- the baby is due: the 15th week before the expected week, a Sunday-to-Saturday week unless
-- regulations prescribe otherwise. The caller names any day in the expected week of birth. This is
-- the answer ssc7's s 171ZZ16 asks for (seam S30).
-- src:3396 | (3) The references in subsection (2) above to the relevant week are to the week immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of the child’s birth.
@ref s 171ZA(3) (src:3396)
GIVEN `a day in the expected week of the child's birth` IS A DATE
      `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 171ZA(2) the relevant week` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 171ZJ(5) the first day of the week containing the day` `a day in the expected week of the child's birth` `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the week is the one "as may be prescribed"
    WHEN JUST expectedWeek THEN
        JUST (Period WITH
                  `the first day` IS expectedWeek MINUS (15 TIMES 7)
                  `the last day` IS expectedWeek MINUS ((14 TIMES 7) PLUS 1))

-- Plain gloss: the entitlement is not changed by the birth, or expected birth, of more than one
-- child from the same pregnancy.
-- src:3397 | (4) A person’s entitlement to ... statutory paternity pay under this section shall not be affected by the birth, or expected birth, of more than one child as a result of the same pregnancy.
@ref s 171ZA(4) (src:3397)
GIVEN `more than one child is born, or expected to be born, as a result of the same pregnancy` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZA(4) the entitlement is affected by the birth, or expected birth, of more than one child` MEANS
    IF `more than one child is born, or expected to be born, as a result of the same pregnancy`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall not be affected by the birth, or expected birth, of more than one child as a result of the same pregnancy"
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection speaks only of more than one child

-- Plain gloss: "newborn child" includes a child stillborn after 24 weeks of pregnancy. It says
-- "includes", so a child stillborn earlier is not ruled out and is NOTHING.
-- src:3398 | (5) In this section, “newborn child” includes a child stillborn after twenty-four weeks of pregnancy.
@ref s 171ZA(5) (src:3398)
GIVEN `the weeks of pregnancy when the child was stillborn` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZA(5) a child stillborn counts as a newborn child` MEANS
    IF `the weeks of pregnancy when the child was stillborn` GREATER THAN `the weeks of pregnancy named in the stillbirth rule`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "“newborn child” includes a child stillborn after twenty-four weeks of pregnancy"

§§ `s 171ZB Entitlement: adoption`

DECLARE `s 171ZB Adoption relationship` IS ONE OF
    `s 171ZB the person with whom the child is placed`
    `s 171ZB the partner of the person with whom the child is placed`

-- src:3402 | (a) that he satisfies prescribed conditions—
-- src:3403 | (i) as to relationship with a child who is placed for adoption under the law of any part of the United Kingdom, and
-- src:3404 | (ii) as to relationship with a person with whom the child is so placed for adoption;
@ref s 171ZB(2)(a) (src:3402-3404)
GIVEN `he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with a child who is placed for adoption under the law of any part of the United Kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN
      `he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with a person with whom the child is so placed for adoption` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(2)(a) he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with the child and with the person with whom the child is placed` MEANS
    IF     `he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with a child who is placed for adoption under the law of any part of the United Kingdom`
       AND `he satisfies prescribed conditions as to relationship with a person with whom the child is so placed for adoption`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are—"

-- src:3405 | (b) that he has been in employed earner’s employment with an employer for a continuous period of at least 26 weeks ending with the relevant week;
@ref s 171ZB(2)(b) (src:3405)
GIVEN service IS A `Service for statutory pay`
      `his employment with the employer is employed earner's employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(2)(b) he has been in employed earner's employment with an employer for a continuous period of at least 26 weeks ending with the relevant week` MEANS
    IF     `his employment with the employer is employed earner's employment`
       AND service's `weeks of continuous employment with the employer ending with the relevant week`
               AT LEAST `s 171ZA(2)(b) the number of weeks of continuous employment required`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are—"

-- src:3406 | (c) that his normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks ending with the relevant week are not less than the lower earnings limit in force under section 5(1)(a) at the end of the relevant week;
@ref s 171ZB(2)(c) (src:3406)
GIVEN service IS A `Service for statutory pay`
      `s 5(1)(a) the lower earnings limit in force at the time in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(2)(c) his normal weekly earnings are not less than the lower earnings limit at the end of the relevant week` MEANS
    IF service's `normal weekly earnings in pounds over the period the section names`
           AT LEAST `s 5(1)(a) the lower earnings limit in force at the time in pounds`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are—"

-- src:3407 | (d) that he has been in employed earner’s employment with the employer by reference to whom the condition in paragraph (b) above is satisfied for a continuous period beginning with the end of the relevant week and ending with the day on which the child is placed for adoption; and
@ref s 171ZB(2)(d) (src:3407)
GIVEN `he has been in employed earner's employment with that employer for a continuous period beginning with the end of the relevant week and ending with the day on which the child is placed for adoption` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(2)(d) he has been in employed earner's employment with that employer from the end of the relevant week to the day the child is placed` MEANS
    IF `he has been in employed earner's employment with that employer for a continuous period beginning with the end of the relevant week and ending with the day on which the child is placed for adoption`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are—"

-- Plain gloss: paragraph (e) applies only to a person with whom the child is placed: he must have
-- elected to receive statutory paternity pay. For the other person (the partner) the condition has
-- nothing to bite on, and is taken as met (fork F-e).
-- src:3408 | (e) where he is a person with whom the child is placed for adoption, that he has elected to receive statutory paternity pay.
@ref s 171ZB(2)(e) (src:3408)
GIVEN relationship IS A `s 171ZB Adoption relationship`
      `he has elected to receive statutory paternity pay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(2)(e) where he is a person with whom the child is placed, he has elected to receive statutory paternity pay` MEANS
    CONSIDER relationship
    WHEN `s 171ZB the partner of the person with whom the child is placed` THEN JUST TRUE        -- the condition is expressed only "where he is a person with whom the child is placed for adoption"
    WHEN `s 171ZB the person with whom the child is placed` THEN
        IF `he has elected to receive statutory paternity pay`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are— ... (e) where he is a person with whom the child is placed for adoption, that he has elected to receive statutory paternity pay"

-- src:3401 | (2) The conditions are—
@ref s 171ZB(2) (src:3401)
GIVEN `condition (a) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `condition (b) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `condition (c) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `condition (d) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `condition (e) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(2) the conditions are satisfied` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions are—"
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): a limb that depends on a figure or fact regulations supply is NOTHING when the caller does not supply it
    `s 171ZA(1) all of these answers hold` (LIST `condition (a) is satisfied`, `condition (b) is satisfied`, `condition (c) is satisfied`, `condition (d) is satisfied`, `condition (e) is satisfied`)

-- src:3400 | (1) Where a person satisfies the conditions in subsection (2) below, he shall be entitled in accordance with the following provisions of this Part to payments to be known as “statutory paternity pay”.
@ref s 171ZB(1) (src:3400)
GIVEN `s 171ZB(2) the conditions are satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(1) he is entitled to statutory paternity pay on an adoption` MEANS
    `s 171ZB(2) the conditions are satisfied`        -- JUST FALSE here is the "no" of subsection (1): "Where a person satisfies the conditions in subsection (2) below, he shall be entitled" (marginal reading (a)); the comments of the conditions quote the text

-- Plain gloss: the "relevant week" for adoption is the week in which the adopter is notified of
-- being matched with the child (for a placement under s 22C or s 81, the week in which the
-- prospective adopter is notified the child is, or is expected to be, placed). A Sunday-to-Saturday
-- week unless regulations prescribe another. This is the answer ssc7's s 171ZZ16 asks for (seam S30).
-- src:3409 | (3) The references in subsection (2) to the relevant week are to the week in which the adopter is notified of being matched with the child for the purposes of adoption.
-- src:3416 | (b) the reference in subsection (3) to the week in which the adopter is notified of being matched with the child for the purposes of adoption is to be treated as a reference to the week in which the prospective adopter is notified that the child is to be, or is expected to be, placed with the prospective adopter under section 22C;
-- src:3422 | (b) the reference in subsection (3) to the week in which the adopter is notified of being matched with the child for the purposes of adoption is to be treated as a reference to the week in which the prospective adopter is notified that the child is to be, or is expected to be, placed with the prospective adopter under section 81;
@ref s 171ZB(3), s 171ZB(8)(b), s 171ZB(10)(b) (src:3409) (src:3416) (src:3422)
GIVEN placement IS A Placement
      `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 171ZB(2) the relevant week` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 171ZJ(5) the first day of the week containing the day` (placement's `the day the person was notified of the match`) `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the week is the one "as may be prescribed"
    WHEN JUST notifiedWeek THEN
        JUST (Period WITH
                  `the first day` IS notifiedWeek
                  `the last day` IS notifiedWeek PLUS 6)

-- Plain gloss: he may not elect to receive statutory paternity pay if he has elected, under s 171ZL, to receive statutory adoption pay.
-- src:3410 | (4) A person may not elect to receive ... statutory paternity pay if he has elected in accordance with section 171ZL below to receive statutory adoption pay.
@ref s 171ZB(4) (src:3410)
GIVEN `he has elected in accordance with section 171ZL to receive statutory adoption pay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(4) he may elect to receive statutory paternity pay` MEANS
    IF `he has elected in accordance with section 171ZL to receive statutory adoption pay`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person may not elect to receive ... statutory paternity pay if he has elected in accordance with section 171ZL below to receive statutory adoption pay."
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection bars an election in one case and does not itself grant it otherwise

-- Plain gloss: the entitlement is not changed by the placement of more than one child as part of the same arrangement.
-- src:3412 | (6) A person’s entitlement to ... statutory paternity pay under this section shall not be affected by the placement for adoption of more than one child as part of the same arrangement.
@ref s 171ZB(6) (src:3412)
GIVEN `more than one child is placed for adoption as part of the same arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(6) the entitlement is affected by the placement for adoption of more than one child` MEANS
    IF `more than one child is placed for adoption as part of the same arrangement`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall not be affected by the placement for adoption of more than one child as part of the same arrangement"
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection speaks only of more than one child

-- Plain gloss: "adopter" is the person by reference to whom he satisfies the condition in (2)(a)(ii).
-- src:3413 | (7) In this section, “adopter”, in relation to a person who satisfies the condition under subsection (2)(a)(ii) above, means the person by reference to whom he satisfies that condition.
-- src:3418 | (d) the definition in subsection (7) is to be treated as if it were a definition of “prospective adopter”.
-- src:3424 | (d) the definition in subsection (7) is to be treated as if it were a definition of “prospective adopter”.
@ref s 171ZB(7), s 171ZB(8)(d), s 171ZB(10)(d) (src:3413) (src:3418) (src:3424)
GIVEN `the person is the one by reference to whom he satisfies the condition under subsection (2)(a)(ii)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(7) the person is the adopter` MEANS
    IF `the person is the one by reference to whom he satisfies the condition under subsection (2)(a)(ii)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“adopter”, in relation to a person who satisfies the condition under subsection (2)(a)(ii) above, means the person by reference to whom he satisfies that condition"

-- Plain gloss: the section also applies to a child placed by a local authority with a local
-- authority foster parent approved as a prospective adopter: in England under s 22C of the Children
-- Act 1989 (subsection (8)), in Wales under s 81 of the Social Services and Well-being (Wales) Act
-- 2014 (subsection (10)). For those the Act's references to placement for adoption are read as
-- references to that placement. A placement of neither kind is outside the modification.
-- src:3414 | (8) This section has effect in a case involving a child placed under section 22C of the Children Act 1989 by a local authority in England with a local authority foster parent who has been approved as a prospective adopter with the following modifications—
-- src:3415 | (a) the references in subsection (2) to a child being placed for adoption under the law of any part of the United Kingdom are to be treated as references to a child being placed under section 22C in that manner;
-- src:3417 | (c) the reference in subsection (6) to placement for adoption is to be treated as a reference to placement under section 22C;
-- src:3420 | (10) This section has effect in a case involving a child placed under section 81 of the Social Services and Well-being (Wales) Act 2014 by a local authority in Wales with a local authority foster parent who has been approved as a prospective adopter with the following modifications—
-- src:3421 | (a) the references in subsection (2) to a child being placed for adoption under the law of any part of the United Kingdom are to be treated as references to a child being placed under section 81 in that manner;
-- src:3423 | (c) the reference in subsection (6) to placement for adoption is to be treated as a reference to placement under section 81;
@ref s 171ZB(8), s 171ZB(10) (src:3414-3415) (src:3417) (src:3420-3421) (src:3423)
GIVEN kind IS A `Kind of placement`
      `the child was placed by a local authority with a local authority foster parent who has been approved as a prospective adopter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(8) the placement is one to which the conditions of subsection (2) are applied` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `placed for adoption under the law of a part of the United Kingdom` THEN JUST TRUE        -- the unmodified case, subsection (2)(a)(i)
    WHEN `placed under section 22C of the Children Act 1989` THEN
        IF `the child was placed by a local authority with a local authority foster parent who has been approved as a prospective adopter`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (8) applies "in a case involving a child placed under section 22C ... with a local authority foster parent who has been approved as a prospective adopter"
    WHEN `placed under section 81 of the Social Services and Well-being (Wales) Act 2014` THEN
        IF `the child was placed by a local authority with a local authority foster parent who has been approved as a prospective adopter`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (10) applies "in a case involving a child placed under section 81 ... with a local authority foster parent who has been approved as a prospective adopter"

-- Plain gloss: a person who has become entitled through a s 22C placement (subsection (9)) or a
-- s 81 placement (subsection (11)) may not also become entitled in connection with the placement of the child for adoption.
-- src:3419 | (9) Where, by virtue of subsection (8), a person becomes entitled to statutory paternity pay in connection with the placement of a child under section 22C of the Children Act 1989, the person may not become entitled to payments of statutory paternity pay in connection with the placement of the child for adoption.
@ref s 171ZB(9) (src:3419)
GIVEN `by virtue of subsection (8) he has become entitled in connection with the placement under section 22C` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(9) he may become entitled in connection with the placement of the child for adoption` MEANS
    IF `by virtue of subsection (8) he has become entitled in connection with the placement under section 22C`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the person may not become entitled to payments of statutory paternity pay in connection with the placement of the child for adoption"
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection speaks only of a person entitled by virtue of subsection (8)

-- src:3425 | (11) Where, by virtue of subsection (10), a person becomes entitled to statutory paternity pay in connection with the placement of a child under section 81 of the Social Services and Well-being (Wales) Act 2014, the person may not become entitled to payments of statutory paternity pay in connection with the placement of the child for adoption.
@ref s 171ZB(11) (src:3425)
GIVEN `by virtue of subsection (10) he has become entitled in connection with the placement under section 81` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZB(11) he may become entitled in connection with the placement of the child for adoption` MEANS
    IF `by virtue of subsection (10) he has become entitled in connection with the placement under section 81`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the person may not become entitled to payments of statutory paternity pay in connection with the placement of the child for adoption"
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection speaks only of a person entitled by virtue of subsection (10)

§§ `s 171ZC Entitlement: general`

-- Plain gloss: notice. He is entitled to payments only if he gives the person who will be liable
-- to pay notice of the week or weeks in respect of which he expects there to be liability. The time
-- limit is for regulations (subsection (1A)).
-- src:3427 | (1) A person shall be entitled to payments of ... statutory paternity pay in respect of any period only if he gives the person who will be liable to pay it notice of the week or weeks in respect of which he expects there to be liability to pay him statutory paternity pay.
@ref s 171ZC(1) (src:3427)
GIVEN `he gives the person who will be liable to pay it notice of the week or weeks in respect of which he expects there to be liability to pay him statutory paternity pay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZC(1) the notice condition for payments of statutory paternity pay is met` MEANS
    IF `he gives the person who will be liable to pay it notice of the week or weeks in respect of which he expects there to be liability to pay him statutory paternity pay`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person shall be entitled to payments of ... statutory paternity pay in respect of any period only if he gives the person who will be liable to pay it notice"

-- Plain gloss: the notice must be in writing if the person who has to pay asks for that.
-- src:3429 | (2) The notice shall be in writing if the person who is liable to pay the ... statutory paternity pay so requests.
@ref s 171ZC(2) (src:3429)
GIVEN `the person who is liable to pay the statutory paternity pay requests it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZC(2) the notice shall be in writing` MEANS
    IF `the person who is liable to pay the statutory paternity pay requests it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The notice shall be in writing if the person who is liable to pay the ... statutory paternity pay so requests." (marginal reading (a): a failed "if" counts as the text's no)

§§ `s 171ZD Liability to make payments`

-- Plain gloss: the person who must pay is any person of whom the entitled person has been an
-- employee in the way s 171ZA(2)(b) and (d), or s 171ZB(2)(b) and (d), describe. The Act names nobody
-- else here; regulations can put the liability on the Board (subsection (3)).
-- src:3444 | (1) The liability to make payments of ... statutory paternity pay under section 171ZA or 171ZB above is a liability of any person of whom the person entitled to the payments has been an employee as mentioned in subsection (2)(b) and (d) of that section.
@ref s 171ZD(1) (src:3444)
GIVEN `the entitled person has been an employee of the person as mentioned in subsection (2)(b) and (d) of section 171ZA or 171ZB` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZD(1) the liability to make payments of statutory paternity pay is the person's` MEANS
    IF `the entitled person has been an employee of the person as mentioned in subsection (2)(b) and (d) of section 171ZA or 171ZB`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection names who is liable, and says nothing of anyone else; subsection (3) can make the Board liable

§§ `s 171ZE Rate and period of pay`

-- Plain gloss: the weekly rate is whatever fixed or earnings-related rate regulations prescribe.
-- The Act states no figure, so the rate is an input and NOTHING stays NOTHING.
-- src:3448 | (1) ... Statutory paternity pay shall be payable at such fixed or earnings-related weekly rate as may be prescribed by regulations, which may prescribe different kinds of rate for different cases.
@ref s 171ZE(1) (src:3448)
GIVEN `the weekly rate prescribed by regulations in pounds` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 171ZE(1) the weekly rate of statutory paternity pay in pounds` MEANS
    `the weekly rate prescribed by regulations in pounds`        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "at such fixed or earnings-related weekly rate as may be prescribed by regulations"

-- Plain gloss: how the weeks of pay may be chosen. (a) two consecutive weeks within the qualifying
-- period, beginning on a date he chooses; (b) if regulations permit, one week, or two separate
-- weeks, within the qualifying period.
DECLARE `s 171ZE Choice of weeks` IS ONE OF
    `s 171ZE two consecutive weeks`
    `s 171ZE one week`
    `s 171ZE two non-consecutive periods of a week`

-- src:3449 | (2) ... Statutory paternity pay shall be payable in respect of—
-- src:3450 | (a) a period of two consecutive weeks within the qualifying period beginning on such date within that period as the person entitled may choose in accordance with regulations, or
-- src:3451 | (b) if regulations permit the person entitled to choose to receive ... statutory paternity pay in respect of—
-- src:3452 | (i) a period of a week, or
-- src:3453 | (ii) two non-consecutive periods of a week,
-- src:3454 | such week or weeks within the qualifying period as he may choose in accordance with regulations.
@ref s 171ZE(2) (src:3449-3454)
GIVEN choice IS A `s 171ZE Choice of weeks`
      `the weeks chosen are within the qualifying period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations permit him to choose to be paid for a week, or two non-consecutive periods of a week` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZE(2) the person may choose the weeks in respect of which statutory paternity pay is payable` MEANS
    CONSIDER choice
    WHEN `s 171ZE two consecutive weeks` THEN
        IF `the weeks chosen are within the qualifying period`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a period of two consecutive weeks within the qualifying period"
    WHEN `s 171ZE one week` THEN
        IF `regulations permit him to choose to be paid for a week, or two non-consecutive periods of a week` AND `the weeks chosen are within the qualifying period`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "if regulations permit"
    WHEN `s 171ZE two non-consecutive periods of a week` THEN
        IF `regulations permit him to choose to be paid for a week, or two non-consecutive periods of a week` AND `the weeks chosen are within the qualifying period`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "if regulations permit"

-- Plain gloss: the qualifying period (the stretch within which the weeks must fall) begins with the
-- date of the child's birth (a birth) or placement for adoption (an adoption); for twins or more, or
-- children placed together, the date of the first. Regulations set its length, which must be at least 56 days.
DECLARE `s 171ZE Case` IS ONE OF
    `s 171ZE a birth`
    `s 171ZE a placement for adoption`

-- src:3455 | (3) For the purposes of subsection (2) above, the qualifying period shall be determined in accordance with regulations, which shall secure that it is a period of at least 56 days beginning—
-- src:3456 | (a) in the case of a person to whom the conditions in section 171ZA(2) above apply, with the date of the child’s birth, and
-- src:3457 | (b) in the case of a person to whom the conditions in section 171ZB(2) above apply, with the date of the child’s placement for adoption.
-- src:3468 | (9) Where more than one child is born as a result of the same pregnancy, the reference in subsection (3)(a) to the date of the child’s birth shall be read as a reference to the date of birth of the first child born as a result of the pregnancy.
-- src:3469 | (10) Where more than one child is placed for adoption as part of the same arrangement, the reference in subsection (3)(b) to the date of the child’s placement shall be read as a reference to the date of placement of the first child to be placed as part of the arrangement.
@ref s 171ZE(3), s 171ZE(9), s 171ZE(10) (src:3455-3457) (src:3468-3469)
GIVEN case IS A `s 171ZE Case`
      `the date of the child's birth, or of the first child born as a result of the same pregnancy` IS A DATE
      `the date of the child's placement for adoption, or of the first child to be placed as part of the same arrangement` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 171ZE(3) the day on which the qualifying period begins` MEANS
    CONSIDER case
    WHEN `s 171ZE a birth` THEN `the date of the child's birth, or of the first child born as a result of the same pregnancy`
    WHEN `s 171ZE a placement for adoption` THEN `the date of the child's placement for adoption, or of the first child to be placed as part of the same arrangement`

-- src:3455 | (3) For the purposes of subsection (2) above, the qualifying period shall be determined in accordance with regulations, which shall secure that it is a period of at least 56 days beginning—
@ref s 171ZE(3) (src:3455)
`s 171ZE(3) the least length of the qualifying period in days` MEANS 56

-- Plain gloss: the qualifying period that regulations determine must be at least 56 days long; one
-- that is shorter does not meet what the subsection requires. The text says what the regulations
-- "shall secure" and not what follows if they do not, so the "no" is the failure of the stated
-- minimum, and the question the rule answers is whether that minimum is met (marginal reading (a)).
@ref s 171ZE(3) (src:3455)
GIVEN `the length of the qualifying period determined in accordance with regulations, in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZE(3) the qualifying period determined by regulations is long enough` MEANS
    IF `the length of the qualifying period determined in accordance with regulations, in days` AT LEAST `s 171ZE(3) the least length of the qualifying period in days`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall secure that it is a period of at least 56 days" (marginal reading (a): a stated minimum the text requires counts as the text's no, as in s 154(3) and s 166(3))

-- Plain gloss: statutory paternity pay is not payable for a week if statutory shared parental pay
-- is payable to him for part of it, or he takes shared parental leave in part of it, or either
-- happened in respect of the child before that week.
-- src:3458 | (3A) Statutory paternity pay is not payable to a person in respect of a statutory pay week if—
-- src:3459 | (a) statutory shared parental pay is payable to that person in respect of any part of that week or that person takes shared parental leave in any part of that week, or
-- src:3460 | (b) statutory shared parental pay was payable to that person or that person has taken shared parental leave in respect of the child before that week.
@ref s 171ZE(3A) (src:3458-3460)
GIVEN `statutory shared parental pay is payable to him in respect of part of that week or he takes shared parental leave in part of that week` IS A BOOLEAN
      `statutory shared parental pay was payable to him or he has taken shared parental leave in respect of the child before that week` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZE(3A) statutory paternity pay is payable to him in respect of the statutory pay week` MEANS
    IF     `statutory shared parental pay is payable to him in respect of part of that week or he takes shared parental leave in part of that week`
        OR `statutory shared parental pay was payable to him or he has taken shared parental leave in respect of the child before that week`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Statutory paternity pay is not payable to a person in respect of a statutory pay week if"
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection bars payment in two cases and does not itself say it is payable otherwise

-- Plain gloss: it is not payable for a week unless, at the start of the week, his purpose is to
-- care for the child or to support the child's mother (or the adopter). Read as "neither purpose"
-- (fork F-purpose).
-- src:3461 | (4) ... Statutory paternity pay shall not be payable to a person in respect of a statutory pay week if it is not his purpose at the beginning of the week—
-- src:3462 | (a) to care for the child by reference to whom he satisfies the condition in sub-paragraph (i) of section 171ZA(2)(a) or 171ZB(2)(a) above, or
-- src:3463 | (b) to support the person by reference to whom he satisfies the condition in sub-paragraph (ii) of that provision.
@ref s 171ZE(4) (src:3461-3463)
GIVEN `at the beginning of the week it is his purpose to care for the child` IS A BOOLEAN
      `at the beginning of the week it is his purpose to support the person by reference to whom he satisfies the condition in sub-paragraph (ii)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZE(4) statutory paternity pay is payable to him in respect of the statutory pay week` MEANS
    IF     (NOT `at the beginning of the week it is his purpose to care for the child`)
       AND (NOT `at the beginning of the week it is his purpose to support the person by reference to whom he satisfies the condition in sub-paragraph (ii)`)
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Statutory paternity pay shall not be payable to a person in respect of a statutory pay week if it is not his purpose at the beginning of the week— (a) to care for the child ... or (b) to support the person"
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection bars payment in one case and does not itself say it is payable otherwise

-- Plain gloss: the employer is not liable to pay a person statutory paternity pay for a week in
-- which that person works for him under a contract of service; whether the contract existed before
-- the week makes no difference (subsection (6): the second input is deliberately unused).
-- src:3464 | (5) A person shall not be liable to pay ... statutory paternity pay to another in respect of a statutory pay week during any part of which the other works under a contract of service with him.
-- src:3465 | (6) It is immaterial for the purposes of subsection (5) above whether the work referred to in that subsection is work under a contract of service which existed immediately before the statutory pay week or a contract of service which did not so exist.
@ref s 171ZE(5), (6) (src:3464-3465)
GIVEN `the other works under a contract of service with him during any part of the statutory pay week` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contract of service existed immediately before the statutory pay week` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZE(5) the person is liable to pay statutory paternity pay to the other in respect of the statutory pay week` MEANS
    IF `the other works under a contract of service with him during any part of the statutory pay week`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person shall not be liable to pay ... statutory paternity pay to another in respect of a statutory pay week during any part of which the other works under a contract of service with him." The second input is immaterial: "It is immaterial ... whether the work ... is work under a contract of service which existed immediately before the statutory pay week or ... which did not so exist."
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection bars liability for a week of work and does not itself say there is liability otherwise

-- Plain gloss: except in prescribed cases, statutory paternity pay is not payable for a week in
-- which he works for an employer who is not liable to pay him statutory paternity pay.
-- src:3466 | (7) Except in such cases as may be prescribed, ... statutory paternity pay shall not be payable to a person in respect of a statutory pay week during any part of which he works for any employer who is not liable to pay him ... statutory paternity pay.
@ref s 171ZE(7) (src:3466)
GIVEN `during part of the statutory pay week he works for an employer who is not liable to pay him statutory paternity pay` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the case is one prescribed under subsection (7)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZE(7) statutory paternity pay is payable to him in respect of the statutory pay week` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `during part of the statutory pay week he works for an employer who is not liable to pay him statutory paternity pay` AND `the case is one prescribed under subsection (7)`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Except in such cases as may be prescribed"
        IF `during part of the statutory pay week he works for an employer who is not liable to pay him statutory paternity pay`
            THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "statutory paternity pay shall not be payable to a person in respect of a statutory pay week during any part of which he works for any employer who is not liable to pay him statutory paternity pay"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection bars payment for such a week and does not itself say it is payable otherwise

-- Plain gloss: a daily rate, where one is needed, is one seventh of the weekly rate.
-- src:3470 | (10A) Where for any purpose of this Part of this Act or of regulations it is necessary to calculate the daily rate of ... statutory paternity pay, the amount payable by way of ... statutory paternity pay for any day shall be taken as one seventh of the weekly rate.
@ref s 171ZE(10A) (src:3470)
GIVEN `the weekly rate of statutory paternity pay in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 171ZE(10A) the daily rate of statutory paternity pay in pounds` MEANS
    `the weekly rate of statutory paternity pay in pounds` DIVIDED BY 7

-- Plain gloss: a "statutory pay week" is a week the person chose as one in respect of which it is payable.
-- src:3471 | (11) In this section—
-- src:3472 | “statutory pay week”, in relation to a person entitled to ... statutory paternity pay, means a week chosen by him as a week in respect of which ... statutory paternity pay shall be payable;
@ref s 171ZE(11) "statutory pay week" (src:3471-3472)
GIVEN `it is a week chosen by him as a week in respect of which statutory paternity pay shall be payable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZE(11) the week is a statutory pay week` MEANS
    IF `it is a week chosen by him as a week in respect of which statutory paternity pay shall be payable`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“statutory pay week”, in relation to a person entitled to ... statutory paternity pay, means a week chosen by him as a week in respect of which ... statutory paternity pay shall be payable"

-- Plain gloss: where pay is payable through a s 22C placement (subsection (12)) or a s 81
-- placement (subsection (13)), the Act's references to "placement for adoption" in subsections (3)(b)
-- and (10) are read as references to that placement.
-- src:3474 | (12) Where statutory paternity pay is payable to a person by virtue of section 171ZB(8), this section has effect as if—
-- src:3475 | (a) the references in subsections (3)(b) and (10) to placement for adoption were references to placement under section 22C of the Children Act 1989;
-- src:3476 | (b) the references in subsection (10) to being placed for adoption were references to being placed under section 22C.
-- src:3477 | (13) Where statutory paternity pay is payable to a person by virtue of section 171ZB(10), this section has effect as if—
-- src:3478 | (a) the references in subsections (3)(b) and (10) to placement for adoption were references to placement under section 81 of the Social Services and Well-being (Wales) Act 2014;
-- src:3479 | (b) the references in subsection (10) to being placed for adoption were references to being placed under section 81.
@ref s 171ZE(12), s 171ZE(13) (src:3474-3476) (src:3477-3479)
GIVEN `statutory paternity pay is payable to him by virtue of section 171ZB(8)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `statutory paternity pay is payable to him by virtue of section 171ZB(10)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of placement`
`s 171ZE(12) the placement that the references to placement for adoption are read as` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `statutory paternity pay is payable to him by virtue of section 171ZB(8)` AND `statutory paternity pay is payable to him by virtue of section 171ZB(10)`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the two subsections each apply to a different placement and the text does not say what happens if both are claimed
        IF `statutory paternity pay is payable to him by virtue of section 171ZB(8)`
            THEN JUST `placed under section 22C of the Children Act 1989`
        IF `statutory paternity pay is payable to him by virtue of section 171ZB(10)`
            THEN JUST `placed under section 81 of the Social Services and Well-being (Wales) Act 2014`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): neither subsection (12) nor (13) modifies the reading

§§ `ss 171ZEA to 171ZEE Additional statutory paternity pay (blank in this text)`

-- The five sections 171ZEA to 171ZEE (src:3481-3490) show only a line of dots in the converter text.
-- They are repealed or blank here; the coverage table says so, and nothing is encoded.

§§ `s 171ZF Restrictions on contracting out`

-- Plain gloss: a contract term cannot take away or cut down statutory paternity pay, or make an
-- employee pay towards the employer's costs of it. "To the extent that it purports" limits the voidness.
-- src:3493 | (1) Any agreement shall be void to the extent that it purports—
-- src:3494 | (a) to exclude, limit or otherwise modify any provision of this Part of this Act, or
-- src:3495 | (b) to require an employee or former employee to contribute (whether directly or indirectly) towards any costs incurred by his employer or former employer under this Part of this Act.
@ref s 171ZF(1) (src:3493-3495)
GIVEN `the agreement purports to exclude, limit or otherwise modify a provision of Part 12ZA` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the agreement purports to require an employee or former employee to contribute, directly or indirectly, towards costs incurred by his employer or former employer under Part 12ZA` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZF(1) the agreement is void to the extent that it purports` MEANS
    IF     `the agreement purports to exclude, limit or otherwise modify a provision of Part 12ZA`
        OR `the agreement purports to require an employee or former employee to contribute, directly or indirectly, towards costs incurred by his employer or former employer under Part 12ZA`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to the extent that it purports"

-- src:3496 | (2) For the avoidance of doubt, any agreement between an employer and an employee authorising any deductions from statutory paternity pay which the employer is liable to pay to the employee in respect of any period shall not be void by virtue of subsection (1)(a) above if the employer—
-- src:3497 | (a) is authorised by that or another agreement to make the same deductions from any contractual remuneration which he is liable to pay in respect of the same period, or
-- src:3498 | (b) would be so authorised if he were liable to pay contractual remuneration in respect of that period.
@ref s 171ZF(2) (src:3496-3498)
GIVEN `the agreement authorises deductions from statutory paternity pay that the employer is liable to pay in respect of a period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer is authorised by that or another agreement to make the same deductions from contractual remuneration he is liable to pay in respect of the same period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer would be so authorised if he were liable to pay contractual remuneration in respect of that period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZF(2) the agreement is protected from being void by virtue of subsection (1)(a)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the agreement authorises deductions from statutory paternity pay that the employer is liable to pay in respect of a period`)
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks only of "any agreement ... authorising any deductions"
        IF    `the employer is authorised by that or another agreement to make the same deductions from contractual remuneration he is liable to pay in respect of the same period`
           OR `the employer would be so authorised if he were liable to pay contractual remuneration in respect of that period`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall not be void by virtue of subsection (1)(a) above if the employer" (marginal reading (a): a failed "if" counts as the text's no)

§§ `s 171ZG Relationship with contractual remuneration`

-- Plain gloss: being entitled to statutory paternity pay does not take away or change what he is
-- owed under his contract of service. Subsections (2) and (3) then say how the two are credited.
-- src:3500 | (1) Subject to subsections (2) and (3) below, any entitlement to statutory paternity pay shall not affect any right of a person in relation to remuneration under any contract of service (“contractual remuneration”).
@ref s 171ZG(1) (src:3500)
GIVEN `he is entitled to statutory paternity pay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZG(1) the entitlement to statutory paternity pay affects his right to contractual remuneration` MEANS
    IF `he is entitled to statutory paternity pay`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "any entitlement to statutory paternity pay shall not affect any right of a person in relation to remuneration under any contract of service" (subject to subsections (2) and (3), which credit one against the other)
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection speaks only of a person entitled to statutory paternity pay

-- Plain gloss: contractual pay the employer has paid for a period counts towards the statutory
-- paternity pay it owes for that period, pound for pound (fork F-credit), so the amount still owed
-- is the liability less what was paid, and never less than nothing.
-- src:3501 | (2) Subject to subsection (3) below—
-- src:3502 | (a) any contractual remuneration paid to a person by an employer of his in respect of any period shall go towards discharging any liability of that employer to pay statutory paternity pay to him in respect of that period; and
@ref s 171ZG(2)(a) (src:3501-3502)
GIVEN `the employer's liability to pay statutory paternity pay for the period, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the contractual remuneration paid to him by the employer in respect of the period, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 171ZG(2)(a) the statutory paternity pay still owed for the period in pounds` MEANS
    max 0 (`the employer's liability to pay statutory paternity pay for the period, in pounds` MINUS `the contractual remuneration paid to him by the employer in respect of the period, in pounds`)

-- Plain gloss: statutory paternity pay paid for a period counts, in the same way, towards the
-- contractual pay the employer owes for that period.
-- src:3501 | (2) Subject to subsection (3) below—
@ref s 171ZG(2)(b) (src:3501)
-- src:3503 | (b) any statutory paternity pay paid by an employer to a person who is an employee of his in respect of any period shall go towards discharging any liability of that employer to pay contractual remuneration to him in respect of that period.
@ref s 171ZG(2)(b) (src:3501) (src:3503)
GIVEN `the employer's liability to pay contractual remuneration for the period, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the statutory paternity pay paid to him by the employer in respect of the period, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 171ZG(2)(b) the contractual remuneration still owed for the period in pounds` MEANS
    max 0 (`the employer's liability to pay contractual remuneration for the period, in pounds` MINUS `the statutory paternity pay paid to him by the employer in respect of the period, in pounds`)

§§ `s 171ZH Crown employment`

-- Plain gloss: a person employed by or under the Crown is treated as any other employee. (Unlike s 161 there is no carve-out for the armed forces.)
-- src:3506 | 171ZH Crown employment-Part 12ZA
-- src:3507 | The provisions of this Part of this Act apply in relation to persons employed by or under the Crown as they apply in relation to persons employed otherwise than by or under the Crown.
@ref s 171ZH (src:3506-3507)
GIVEN `the person is employed by or under the Crown` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZH Part 12ZA applies to the person as it applies to persons employed otherwise than by or under the Crown` MEANS
    IF `the person is employed by or under the Crown`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the section speaks only of persons employed by or under the Crown

§§ `s 171ZI Special classes of person`

-- Plain gloss: regulations can change Part 12ZA for a person employed on a ship, vessel, hovercraft
-- or aircraft, or outside Great Britain at a prescribed time, or in prescribed employment on the
-- continental shelf. The power is machinery; what it means to the person is whether they are in a
-- class it can reach. Seam S25 supplies the s 120(2) definition.
-- src:3509 | (1) The Secretary of State may with the concurrence of the Treasury make regulations modifying any provision of this Part of this Act in such manner as he thinks proper in its application to any person who is, has been or is to be—
-- src:3510 | (a) employed on board any ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft;
-- src:3511 | (b) outside Great Britain at any prescribed time or in any prescribed circumstances; or
-- src:3512 | (c) in prescribed employment in connection with continental shelf operations, as defined in section 120(2) above.
@ref s 171ZI(1) (src:3509-3512)
GIVEN `the person is employed on board a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is outside Great Britain at a prescribed time or in prescribed circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 120(2) the employment is in connection with continental shelf operations` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employment is prescribed employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZI(1) regulations may modify Part 12ZA in its application to the person` MEANS
    IF     `the person is employed on board a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft`
        OR `the person is outside Great Britain at a prescribed time or in prescribed circumstances`
        OR (`the employment is prescribed employment` AND `s 120(2) the employment is in connection with continental shelf operations`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power reaches persons "employed on board any ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft", "outside Great Britain" or in "prescribed employment in connection with continental shelf operations"

§§ `s 171ZJ Part 12ZA: supplementary`

-- Plain gloss: "the Board" is the Commissioners of Inland Revenue.
-- src:3520 | “the Board” means the Commissioners of Inland Revenue;
@ref s 171ZJ(1) "the Board" (src:3520)
`s 171ZJ(1) the Board` MEANS `the Commissioners of Inland Revenue`

-- Plain gloss: the "employer" of an employee is the person who, under s 6, has to pay secondary
-- Class 1 contributions on the employee's earnings, or who would have to but for the s 6(1)(b)
-- condition or because the employee is under 16. Whether s 6 makes someone liable is ssc1's answer.
-- src:3521 | “employer”, in relation to a person who is an employee, means a person who—
-- src:3522 | under section 6 above is, liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of the earnings of the person who is an employee; or
-- src:3523 | would be liable to pay such contributions but for—
-- src:3524 | the condition in section 6(1)(b), or
-- src:3525 | the employee being under the age of 16;
@ref s 171ZJ(1) "employer" (src:3521-3525)
GIVEN `s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of the earnings of the person who is an employee` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the condition in section 6(1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the employee being under the age of 16` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 173(a) the person is under 16` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZJ(1) the person is the employer in relation to the employee` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (`s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of the earnings of the person who is an employee` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the condition in section 6(1)(b)`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the employee being under the age of 16` AND (`s 173(a) the person is under 16` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF (`s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of the earnings of the person who is an employee` EQUALS NOTHING)
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether s 6 makes the person liable is ssc1's answer
        IF `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the employee being under the age of 16` AND (`s 173(a) the person is under 16` EQUALS NOTHING)
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the employee is under 16 is s 173(a)'s answer
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employer”, in relation to a person who is an employee, means a person who"

-- Plain gloss: "local authority" and "local authority foster parent" have the meanings the Children
-- Act 1989 gives them (s 105(1) of that Act). That Act is not among the sources, so each is asked as a fact.
-- src:3526 | “local authority” has the same meaning as in the Children Act 1989 (see section 105(1) of that Act);
-- src:3527 | “local authority foster parent” has the same meaning as in the Children Act 1989 (see section 105(1) of that Act);
@ref s 171ZJ(1) "local authority", "local authority foster parent" (src:3526-3527)
GIVEN `the body is a local authority within the meaning of the Children Act 1989 (section 105(1))` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZJ(1) the body is a local authority` MEANS
    `the body is a local authority within the meaning of the Children Act 1989 (section 105(1))`        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "has the same meaning as in the Children Act 1989"

GIVEN `the person is a local authority foster parent within the meaning of the Children Act 1989 (section 105(1))` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZJ(1) the person is a local authority foster parent` MEANS
    `the person is a local authority foster parent within the meaning of the Children Act 1989 (section 105(1))`        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "has the same meaning as in the Children Act 1989"

-- Plain gloss: "modifications" includes additions, omissions and amendments. It says "includes",
-- so another kind of change is not ruled out and is NOTHING.
DECLARE `s 171ZJ(1) Kind of change` IS ONE OF
    `s 171ZJ(1) an addition`
    `s 171ZJ(1) an omission`
    `s 171ZJ(1) an amendment`
    `s 171ZJ(1) another kind of change`

-- src:3528 | “modifications” includes additions, omissions and amendments, and related expressions are to be read accordingly;
@ref s 171ZJ(1) "modifications" (src:3528)
GIVEN change IS A `s 171ZJ(1) Kind of change`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZJ(1) the change is a modification` MEANS
    CONSIDER change
    WHEN `s 171ZJ(1) an addition` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 171ZJ(1) an omission` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 171ZJ(1) an amendment` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 171ZJ(1) another kind of change` THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "“modifications” includes additions, omissions and amendments"

-- Plain gloss: in Part 12ZA "prescribed" means prescribed by regulations.
-- src:3529 | “prescribed” means prescribed by regulations.
@ref s 171ZJ(1) "prescribed" (src:3529)
GIVEN `it is prescribed by regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZJ(1) the thing is prescribed` MEANS
    IF `it is prescribed by regulations`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“prescribed” means prescribed by regulations"

-- Plain gloss: an "employee" is a person gainfully employed in Great Britain, under a contract of
-- service or in an office, with earnings (as Parts 1 to 5 use that word); but regulations (subsection
-- (3)) can exclude some such persons and include some others. The converter shows "..." and a blank
-- paragraph (b) (src:3531-3532): words and a limb repealed.
-- src:3530 | (2) In this Part of this Act, “employee” means a person who is—
-- src:3531 | (a) gainfully employed in Great Britain either under a contract of service or in an office (including elective office) with earnings (within the meaning of Parts 1 to 5 above); ...
-- src:3532 | (b) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:3533 | (3) Regulations may provide—
-- src:3534 | (a) for cases where a person who falls within the definition in subsection (2) above is not to be treated as an employee for the purposes of this Part of this Act, and
-- src:3535 | (b) for cases where a person who would not otherwise be an employee for the purposes of this Part of this Act is to be treated as an employee for those purposes.
@ref s 171ZJ(2), (3) (src:3530-3535)
GIVEN employment IS AN Employment
      `the person is gainfully employed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the work is carried on in the territorial waters adjacent to Great Britain` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 172(a) a reference to Great Britain includes the place` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the employment is with earnings within the meaning of Parts 1 to 5` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide that the person is not to be treated as an employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide that the person is to be treated as an employee` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171ZJ(2) the person is an employee` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `regulations provide that the person is not to be treated as an employee` AND `regulations provide that the person is to be treated as an employee`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the two kinds of regulation cannot both apply to one person, and the text does not say which prevails
        IF `regulations provide that the person is to be treated as an employee`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `regulations provide that the person is not to be treated as an employee`
            THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "for cases where a person who falls within the definition in subsection (2) above is not to be treated as an employee for the purposes of this Part of this Act"
        OTHERWISE definition
    WHERE
        inGreatBritain MEANS
            IF employment's `carried on in Great Britain`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE IF `the work is carried on in the territorial waters adjacent to Great Britain`
                 THEN `s 172(a) a reference to Great Britain includes the place`
                 ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a person who is gainfully employed in Great Britain" (work outside Great Britain and outside the adjacent waters)
        restOfDefinition MEANS
                `the person is gainfully employed`
            AND (employment's `held under a contract of service` OR employment's `held in an office`)
            AND `the employment is with earnings within the meaning of Parts 1 to 5`
        definition MEANS
            CONSIDER inGreatBritain
            WHEN NOTHING THEN
                IF restOfDefinition
                THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the adjacent waters are in Great Britain is s 172(a)'s answer
                ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a person who is gainfully employed in Great Britain" (a limb other than the place already fails)
            WHEN JUST inside THEN
                IF inside AND restOfDefinition
                THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a person who is— (a) gainfully employed in Great Britain either under a contract of service or in an office (including elective office) with earnings"

-- Plain gloss: normal weekly earnings are the average weekly earnings paid to him, or for his
-- benefit, under the contract of service with the employer in question during the "relevant
-- period" (subsections (6) and (7)); in prescribed cases regulations calculate them instead (subsection (8)).
-- src:3540 | (6) For the purposes of this Part of this Act, a person’s normal weekly earnings shall, subject to subsection (8) below, be taken to be the average weekly earnings which in the relevant period have been paid to him or paid for his benefit under the contract of service with the employer in question.
-- src:3541 | (7) For the purposes of subsection (6) above, “earnings” and “relevant period” shall have the meanings given to them by regulations.
-- src:3542 | (8) In such cases as may be prescribed, a person’s normal weekly earnings shall be calculated in accordance with regulations.
@ref s 171ZJ(6), (7), (8) (src:3540-3542)
GIVEN `the earnings paid to him or for his benefit under the contract of service with the employer in question in the relevant period, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of weeks in the relevant period` IS A NUMBER
      `the case is one in which regulations require his normal weekly earnings to be calculated in accordance with regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 171ZJ(6) his normal weekly earnings in pounds` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the case is one in which regulations require his normal weekly earnings to be calculated in accordance with regulations`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "In such cases as may be prescribed, a person's normal weekly earnings shall be calculated in accordance with regulations"
        IF `the number of weeks in the relevant period` AT MOST 0
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "relevant period" has the meaning regulations give it; a period of no weeks has no average
        OTHERWISE JUST (`the earnings paid to him or for his benefit under the contract of service with the employer in question in the relevant period, in pounds` DIVIDED BY `the number of weeks in the relevant period`)
